# No Git Commit or Push

**Never** run `git commit`, `git push`, or any command that creates a commit or publishes to a remote (including `git commit --amend`, `git push --force`, etc.).

- Do not stage-and-commit, even if the user asks you to "save" work or finish a task.
- Do not push branches or tags.
- Read-only git commands (`status`, `diff`, `log`, `branch`) are fine when needed for context.
- The **user** decides when to commit or push. If they want a commit or push, they will do it themselves or give an explicit instruction in that turn — and even then, prefer confirming rather than assuming.

If the user asks you to commit or push, remind them that project rules forbid the agent from doing so, and offer a suggested commit message or push command they can run.
